module std.coordinate_frame


// A COORDINATE FRAME'S IDENTITY, CARRIED BY THE VALUES THAT LIVE IN IT.
//
// This module exists because of a measurement, and the measurement is the whole justification for
// its shape. SPATIAL-2 modelled the frame as a phantom type parameter - `Point3<RoomFrame>` - on the
// stated ground that a cross-frame subtraction "does not typecheck", which was called the stronger
// of the two available options. It was measured on the production compile-and-run path on
// 2026-08-11 and it is false: a `Point3<RoomFrame>` and a `Point3<BuildingFrame>` flowed through
// `displacement_between3` and the program compiled clean and returned 9. Not a refusal, not a
// crash - a plausible number for a question that has no answer, which is precisely the fabricated
// output DESIGN 5 forbids.
//
// The cause is a v1 seed limitation, not a modelling slip. `v1.compiler.infer`'s argument-position
// judge compares nominal HEAD NAMES; applied type arguments live in the type node's children and
// every name-level comparison discards them. `Point3` equals `Point3`, so the frames were never
// compared. The repair to that judge (`applied_nominal_call_arg_mismatch`) lands alongside this
// module and does close the call-boundary case - but it CANNOT close the producer side, because
// return-position conformance is a separate relation that declines to judge record types at all,
// blocked on `feature:conformance-produced-type-identity`. So this stays writable regardless:
//
//     fn falsely_reframe(p: Point3<RoomFrame>) -> Point3<BuildingFrame> { p }
//
// A consumer receiving that value sees only the declared type. It cannot reconstruct an identity the
// producer already erased. THEREFORE THE CARRIED IDENTITY IS THE AUTHORITY, and the honest claim is
// frame consistency BY CHECKED DERIVATION, not by construction. Every frame-sensitive operation
// compares the identities its operands actually carry and returns a typed mismatch refusal - which
// survives a lying return annotation, because the value still says where it came from.
//
// WHY NOT KEEP THE PHANTOM TOO. Two authorities for one fact is the DESIGN 3 violation whichever one
// is right, and the weaker of the two here is the one the compiler cannot enforce end-to-end. A
// phantom retained beside an authoritative carried identity invites exactly the reading that just
// cost a merged PR its central claim: that the type parameter is doing work. It is removed rather
// than demoted. Dissolve-on: when `feature:conformance-produced-type-identity` closes, a static
// frame projection can be reintroduced ABOVE this carrier and promoted to the construction rung,
// and the carried check remains as the serialization / external-input boundary where a static
// guarantee cannot reach anyway.
//
// IDENTITY IS MINTED AND DECLARATION-OWNED, never derived from a name, a path, or the contents of
// the frame. That follows the ruling already recorded for `gunbc.host_resource`
// `HostResourceIdentity`: a content-derived key makes two coincidentally-identical frames one frame,
// which is the heuristic DESIGN 4 rules unnecessary in a closed system. A frame's human-facing name
// is a display fact and belongs on whatever registers frames; it is deliberately absent here so it
// cannot be mistaken for the identity.
type CoordinateFrameIdentity {
  occurrence: Int
}

// THIRD INSTANCE OF ONE SHAPE, recorded rather than abstracted. `std.occurrence_identity` mints
// SOURCE-occurrence identities, `gunbc.host_resource` mints host-resource identities, and this mints
// coordinate-frame identities; all three are "a minted Int occurrence in a subject-specific space".
// The subject-parameterized carrier that would unify them is already a registered trigger on the
// fleet-reconcile lane, and it stays a trigger rather than being built here: sharing one Int space
// across subjects is what would let `coordinate_frame_identity_eq` answer true on a numeric
// coincidence with an unrelated subject, the cross-family comparison the ContentHash family
// grounding closed. What this row adds is evidence the extraction is due - three subjects, not two.
data coordinate_frame_identity_extraction_note: String = "std.coordinate_frame CoordinateFrameIdentity is the third minted-occurrence identity carrier in the corpus, after std.occurrence_identity (source occurrences) and gunbc.host_resource HostResourceIdentity (host resources). The subject-parameterized extraction is registered on the membership-diff reconcile spine lane as a trigger, not built here. Do not merge the Int spaces without the subject parameter: equality across two subjects sharing one space answers true on a numeric coincidence."

fn coordinate_frame_identity(occurrence: Int) -> CoordinateFrameIdentity {
  CoordinateFrameIdentity { occurrence: occurrence }
}

fn coordinate_frame_identity_eq(a: CoordinateFrameIdentity, b: CoordinateFrameIdentity) -> Bool {
  a.occurrence == b.occurrence
}
